From: EglantinePS5 <noreply@eglantineps5.local>
Subject: [PATCH] EDEN_UP_SWIZZLE_UNITS=1: block-linear data is copied in 16-byte units from a table (Lichii PR #784, as Frizti/Fritchii db3fdb2927)

Making a new texture's block-linear data plain (and the other way round) worked out every unit's
place with two bit deposits. Upstream copies 16-byte units whose place in a GOB comes from a
32-entry table (8 rows by 4 units): 1024x1024 RGBA8 went from 15.8 to 21.8 GB/s on a PC there.
The sub-rectangle copies do the same for pixel sizes that are a power of two, in runs of whole
units with the partial ones at a row's ends.

Upstream has this always on. Here: off unless the switch is exactly "1" (one log line says so at
its first use), and with it off the code runs as before. It works together with EDEN_UP_UNSWIZZLE_THREADS (patch 0542): the unit loop is a
second body for the same line dispatch, so a large unswizzle is both split over the helpers and
copied in units.

Checked off the console before the first build: the function bodies of this file, built with
address and undefined-behaviour checks, give the same bytes with the switch on as with it off in
20,982 cases (all sizes of full copies with 16-byte pixels in both directions, with and without
the line threads, both tilings, block heights 0-4, block depths 0-2; 6,000 random sub-rectangles
of 1, 2, 4, 8 and 16-byte pixels).

